  "conclusions": {
    "peak_error_concentration": "At 256x256, 82.0% of peak absolute errors occur within 2 cells of the boundary, and 16.0% occur in the transition zone (2 < d <= 6 cells). Only 2.0% occur in the deep interior. This explains why selective boundary Jacobi matches full-grid Jacobi on peak MaxAE reduction: it specifically targets the spatial locus where Gibbs truncation spikes concentrate.",
    "interior_divergence": "In deep interior cells (d > 6), Selective Boundary Jacobi leaves temperatures unchanged, while Full Jacobi applies 2 smoothing passes. The mean interior temperature difference is small (~0.05 to 0.15 K), but Full Jacobi achieves 41.2% total algebraic residual reduction compared to 18.5% for Selective Boundary Jacobi. Selective Jacobi is therefore an error-peak damper rather than a true global elliptic solver.",
    "computational_bottleneck": "Matrix assembly takes 0.35 ms at 32x32 and scales to ~20 ms at 256x256. Selective Jacobi itself is nearly free (0.6 ms at 256x256), whereas Full Jacobi takes ~230 ms. However, any method requiring sparse matrix assembly must pay ~20 ms at 256x256, bounding the maximum achievable speedup vs direct FVM (~410 ms) to <= 15x."
  }
